<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoraPackageName>Whiteboard</AgoraPackageName>
    <!--
      com.github.netless-io:whiteboard-android is the Interactive Whiteboard SDK. Agora acquired
      the product and documents it, but the source and the releases are netless-io's, published to
      JitPack — which builds a GitHub tag on demand rather than hosting a release, so there is no
      Maven Central coordinate to point at. Hence the Repository URL; see src/Agora.Binding.props.

      It is a WebView SDK rather than a native one: the .aar carries no .so at all, only Java and
      a JS bundle it loads into a WKWebView-equivalent. That is why it coexists with every other
      package here — nothing to collide with.
    -->
    <AgoraGroupId>com.github.netless-io</AgoraGroupId>
    <AgoraArtifact>whiteboard-android</AgoraArtifact>
    <AgoraNativeVersion>$(AgoraWhiteboardVersion)</AgoraNativeVersion>
    <AgoraMavenRepository>https://jitpack.io</AgoraMavenRepository>
    <VersionPrefix>$(AgoraWhiteboardVersion).$(AgoraWhiteboardBindingRevision)</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="../Agora.Binding.props" />

  <!--
    Unlike every other product here, whiteboard-android has real third-party dependencies rather
    than one Agora infra artifact — and Microsoft publishes bindings for all but one of them, so
    they come in as ordinary PackageReferences rather than as embedded artifacts. That matters
    beyond tidiness: embedding the raw .aars would put a second copy of okio, kotlin-stdlib and
    androidx.core into any app that already has them, and duplicate classes fail the dex merge.

    Square.OkHttp3 pulls in okio and the Kotlin runtime itself, which is why they are not listed
    separately; Java dependency verification (XA4242) is what proves the set is complete.

    The versions are not the newest published. Each of these lines dropped net8.0-android34.0 in
    its most recent release, and this repository still ships net8 deliberately — so the pins are
    the newest of each that still carries a net8 asset. They also carry net10 but no net9, which
    NuGet resolves by falling back to net8 for the middle band.

    WebKit is the exception, on net8 only. MAUI 8.0.100, the last MAUI for net8, still asks for the
    -ktx packages from before AndroidX moved their classes into the base artifacts. WebKit 1.8.0.2
    and later raise Core far enough to pull Collection past that move, and from 1.11.0.3 on
    Lifecycle.Runtime too, so the same classes reach the app twice and D8 fails with "Type
    androidx.collection.ArrayMapKt is defined multiple times". That rules out every WebKit that has
    a net8 asset. The POM asks for webkit 1.1.0, and NuGet's 1.1.0 is MonoAndroid only, so this is
    the newest revision of the lowest line that has a .NET asset. It stays below MAUI 8's Core, so
    it raises nothing in a MAUI app. net9 and net10 keep the newer pin: MAUI 9 and 10 ask for the
    empty -ktx packages themselves. PackageLayoutTests holds the net8 pin below that limit.

    Compared literally for the reason given in src/Agora.Binding.props.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoraWebKitVersion>1.14.0.1</AgoraWebKitVersion>
    <AgoraWebKitVersion Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0-android34.0' ">1.4.0.8</AgoraWebKitVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Square.OkHttp3" Version="5.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="GoogleGson" Version="2.13.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.WebKit" Version="$(AgoraWebKitVersion)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Annotation" Version="1.9.1.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The exception: DSBridge-Android is netless's own fork of a JS-bridge library, published only to
    JitPack under a tag rather than a version ("nl_dsb_v3.0.4"). No NuGet binding exists, so it
    travels inside this package.

    Bound rather than carried, unlike every other extra artifact in this repository. WhiteboardView
    — the board itself, and the one type an app cannot avoid — extends
    wendu.dsbridge.special.DWebView, so leaving DSBridge unbound makes class-parse drop the view
    and every method that takes one, silently: the package still builds, and Agora.Whiteboard.
    WhiteSdk arrives with no constructor. java-resolution-report.log is where that shows up.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AgoraMavenArtifact Include="com.github.netless-io:DSBridge-Android"
                        Version="nl_dsb_v3.0.4"
                        Repository="https://jitpack.io"
                        Bind="true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The Java package is com.herewhite.sdk — netless's original company name, which the SDK never
    renamed. Renamed to Agora.Whiteboard, matching how the other products here present themselves
    and how Agora's own documentation names the product.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AndroidNamespaceReplacement Include="Com.Herewhite.Sdk" Replacement="Agora.Whiteboard" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>.NET for Android / .NET MAUI bindings for the Agora Interactive Whiteboard Android SDK, generated from com.github.netless-io:whiteboard-android $(AgoraWhiteboardVersion) on JitPack. Lets a .NET app join a whiteboard room and draw on it. A WebView SDK, so it coexists with every other Net.Agora package.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);Whiteboard;drawing;collaboration</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
